    {
      "id": "normals-and-topology",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Normals and topology"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Primitive Sphere",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-primitive-sphere"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Primitive Box",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-primitive-box"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A smooth mesh needs coherent normals and consistent winding. Flat-shaded solids require face-specific vertices/normals at sharp edges. Tubes use a transported frame along their centerline so the cross-section does not twist arbitrarily at each sample. Closed paths require a seam treatment; tight bends can produce overlapping tube geometry. Increase sampling until visible curvature is stable, then profile its memory and shading cost."
        }
      ]
    },
